+summary. What is new here is that it is exhibited at the call boundary itself, on captured states,
+rather than inferred from the shape of the predicate. It is a **contract defect, not a harness
+defect**, and it is deliberately not repaired: `postCopy` and `preCopy` are left exactly as the
+catalog states them. The live arm never takes this path — both real destinations are outside the
+image (stack and heap) — so no row above is affected. -/
+theorem copy_destination_inside_code_makes_postCopy_unsatisfiable :
+    copyCodeDestinationContradiction? =
+      some (some "postCopy.MemoryBytes(after,destination)",
+            some "postCopy.CodeIntact(after)", false, true) := by native_decide
+
+/-! ## The results family: what the wiring produced, and why it is unexercised
+
+Everything below is about indices 137 (`rawError`) and 138 (`rawResult`). Two facts frame it:
+
+* they are **emitted**, the property that makes a register question meaningful at all — and the
+  reason the other 127 rows' `a0`/`a1`/`a2` mean nothing;
+* they are **never entered on this corpus arm**, so the wiring produces no verdict about them. That
+  is the honest deliverable, not a shortfall to paper over: the corpus arm that would reach them is
+  one whose *host harness calls the accessors after `zesu_decode_raw` returns*, and **not** a
+  different SSZ input: `results_family_are_call_graph_roots` pins that both are call-graph roots no
+  instance in the program references, so no choice of corpus case can make the decode reach them. -/
+
+/-- The two accessors' place in the generated call graph: parent, children, external calls, and
+absorbed routines. -/
+def resultsFamilyCallGraph : Array (Nat × Option Nat × Array Nat × Array Nat × Array Nat) :=
+  #[137, 138].map fun index =>
+    let row := generatedManifest[index]!
+    (index, row.parent, row.children, row.externalCalls, row.absorbed)
+
+/-- Every function instance naming 137 or 138 as a child, an external call, or an absorbed routine. -/
+def resultsFamilyCallers : Array Nat :=
+  (generatedManifest.filter fun row =>
+    (row.children ++ row.externalCalls ++ row.absorbed).any fun index =>
+      index == 137 || index == 138).map (·.index)
+
+set_option synthInstance.maxSize 400 in
+/-- **This is why no SSZ input can reach either accessor: they are call-graph roots.** Both have no
+parent, no children, no external calls and nothing absorbed, and *no* instance in the program names
+either of them. `zesu_decode_raw` therefore cannot call them however the input is chosen, so no
+corpus case extends the capture to their entry pcs.
